module

public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.Separable
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.DiscreteValuationRing.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.FractionRing
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.ResidueField.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Subalgebra.Lattice

/-! # The local residue conclusion of Abhyankar's lemma

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BRM and 09E7.
(blueprint: lem-sbc-dvr-abhyankar-local-residue) -/

@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace RingTheory

/-- Abhyankar's lemma for a commutative diagram of DVRs and their fraction fields.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0BRM, and its residue-field consequence in Tag 09E7.
(blueprint: lem-sbc-dvr-abhyankar-local-residue)

Proof: Identify A with the normalization of B in Frac(A), using finiteness,
faithfulness and normality. The first ideal identity gives index n, and the
second gives index e for B -> U. The maps into F identify it with the field
compositum of E = Frac(U) and Frac(A); generation excludes a further field
extension. The local map U -> T identifies T with the appropriate local
normalization in this compositum, since a DVR in a fixed fraction field is
the valuation ring selected by its center. Apply 0BRM to these local DVRs.
Its hypotheses hold because e is a unit, e divides n, and the residue
extension for B -> U is separable. Here is the root calculation in 0BRM:
write a uniformizer of B as v times a uniformizer of A to the n-th power;
adjoin an e-th root of the unit v by a finite etale extension. After this
extension, the uniformizer of B has an e-th root in the base fraction field.
First pass to the intermediate field generated by that root. In this
root extension, dividing the root by a uniformizer of U adjoins
an e-th root of a unit, again finite etale. Ramification indices multiply,
so the resulting index over the new base is one; the residue fields are
separable by transitivity. Tag 09EQ preserves these two properties under
the further base change from the intermediate root field to Frac(A).
Descend along the finite etale unit-root extension, as in 0BRM.
This gives adic formal smoothness of A -> T.
Finally 09E7 gives separability for its induced local residue-field map. -/
theorem dvr_abhyankar_local_residue_isSeparable
    (B : Type u) [CommRing B] [IsDomain B] [IsDiscreteValuationRing B]
    (n : ℕ) (hnpos : 0 < n)
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    [Algebra B A] [Module.Finite B A] [FaithfulSMul B A]
    (hindex : (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal B).map (algebraMap B A) =
      IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A ^ n)
    (U : Type u) [CommRing U] [IsDomain U] [IsDiscreteValuationRing U]
    [Algebra B U] [IsLocalHom (algebraMap B U)]
    (e : ℕ) (he : IsUnit (e : B)) (hedvd : e ∣ n)
    (hindexU : (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal B).map (algebraMap B U) =
      IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal U ^ e)
    (hsep : Algebra.IsSeparable (IsLocalRing.ResidueField B) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField U))
    (E : Type u) [Field E] [Algebra (FractionRing B) E]
    [Algebra B E] [IsScalarTower B (FractionRing B) E]
    [FiniteDimensional (FractionRing B) E] [Algebra.IsSeparable (FractionRing B) E]
    [Algebra U E] [IsFractionRing U E] [IsScalarTower B U E]
    (F : Type u) [Field F] [Algebra A F] (iE : E →+* F)
    (hcomm : ∀ b : B, iE (algebraMap B E b) =
      algebraMap A F (algebraMap B A b))
    (hgen : Algebra.adjoin A (Set.range iE) = ⊤)
    (T : Type u) [CommRing T] [IsDomain T] [IsDiscreteValuationRing T]
    [Algebra A T] [IsLocalHom (algebraMap A T)]
    [Algebra T F] [IsFractionRing T F] [IsScalarTower A T F]
    (j : U →+* T) (hj : IsLocalHom j)
    (hjB : ∀ b : B, j (algebraMap B U b) =
      algebraMap A T (algebraMap B A b))
    (hjE : ∀ c : U, algebraMap T F (j c) = iE (algebraMap U E c)) :
    Algebra.IsSeparable (IsLocalRing.ResidueField A) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField T) := by sorry

end RingTheory
